use std
into lob.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with lb_ is exported). It is the vector algebra of the LOBPCG eigen solver in cad/fea.js: a pool of vectors that all have one length, mass-weighted and plain dot products, deflation, block orthogonalization, the Rayleigh-Ritz step with a cyclic Jacobi eigen solver, residuals and the seeded random start. Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript, so every sum runs in the index order written here and every product is grouped exactly as written.

```amni
extern fn lb_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "lb_view_f64";
extern fn lb_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "lb_view_i32";
extern fn lb_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "lb_view_u8";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `N` (vector length), `ND` (number of deflation vectors), `SD` (random seed). Growable arrays: f64 `VP` (the pool; slot s holds the vector whose entry i is `VP`[s·`N` + i]), `MV` (mass per entry), `DG` (diagonal per entry), `GM`, `EV`, `CC` (square matrices stored by rows), `LM` (eigenvalues), `RS` (residual norms); u8 `FX` (fixed flag per entry); i32 `DF` (slots of the deflation vectors), `L1`, `L2`, `L3`, `L4` (slot lists written by JavaScript), `KO` (kept positions), `OD` (eigenvalue order), `SW` (sort scratch).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n int)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries of 0.0. `zi(xs, n int, x int)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `zu(xs, n int)` takes a u8 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n zeros.

`jmax(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.max of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is b when a is negative zero (1.0 divided by a is below 0) and a otherwise; otherwise the larger of a and b. `bad(x real) bool` is true when x equals 0.0 or x is NaN (x differs from itself).

Slice kernels. Each of these loops i from 0 below the length of its first slice. `kmd(xa, xb, xm) real` (three read-only real slices): s starts at 0.0 and becomes s plus (xa[i] times xb[i]) times xm[i]; it returns s. `kdt(xa, xb) real` (two read-only real slices): s starts at 0.0 and becomes s plus xa[i] times xb[i]; it returns s. `kax(ys, c, xs)` (ys a writable real slice, c real, xs a read-only real slice): ys[i] becomes ys[i] minus c times xs[i]. `kad(ys, c, xs)` (same kinds): ys[i] becomes ys[i] plus c times xs[i]. `kdv(ys, d)` (ys a writable real slice, d real): ys[i] becomes ys[i] divided by d. `kdw(ys, ds)` (ys writable, ds a read-only real slice): ys[i] becomes ys[i] divided by ds[i]. `kcp(ys, xs)` (ys writable, xs a read-only real slice): ys[i] becomes xs[i]. `kz(ys)` (a writable real slice): ys[i] becomes 0.0. `krs(rs, a0, xs, ms, l)` (rs writable; a0, xs and ms read-only real slices; l real): rs[i] becomes a0[i] minus (l times ms[i]) times xs[i]. `knr(rs, ms) real` (two read-only real slices): s starts at 0.0 and, for each i where bad(ms[i]) is false, becomes s plus (rs[i] times rs[i]) divided by ms[i]; it returns s.

Slot helpers. `sl(s int)` returns a writable real slice: the part of `VP` from index s·`N` up to but not including s·`N` plus `N`. `mvs()` returns all of `MV` as a real slice and `dgs()` all of `DG`. `md(a int, b int) real` is kmd(sl(a), sl(b), mvs()). `dt(a int, b int) real` is kdt(sl(a), sl(b)). `ax(y int, c real, x int)` calls kax(sl(y), c, sl(x)).

`lb_init(n i32, cap i32, nd i32, ml i32, dg i32)` is exported. It sets `N` to n, `ND` to nd and `SD` to 7, then sizes: zf(`VP`, cap·n), zf(`MV`, n), zu(`FX`, n), zf(`DG`, n when dg is not 0 and 0 otherwise), zi(`DF`, nd, 0), zi of each of `L1`, `L2`, `L3`, `L4`, `KO`, `OD` and `SW` to ml entries of 0, zf of each of `GM`, `EV` and `CC` to ml·ml, zf(`LM`, ml) and zf(`RS`, ml). Then it reports the views: lb_view_f64(1, `VP`), lb_view_f64(2, `MV`), lb_view_u8(3, `FX`), lb_view_i32(4, `DF`), lb_view_i32(5, `L1`), lb_view_i32(6, `L2`), lb_view_i32(7, `L3`), lb_view_i32(8, `L4`), lb_view_f64(9, `LM`), lb_view_f64(10, `RS`), lb_view_i32(11, `KO`), lb_view_f64(12, `DG`).

`lb_fx(s i32)` is exported: for i from 0 below `N`, when `FX`[i] is not 0, `VP`[s·`N` + i] becomes 0.0. `lb_cl(s i32)` is exported: it calls lb_fx(s), then for d from 0 below `ND`, with r equal to `DF`[d] as int, c is md(r, s) and it calls ax(s, c, r). `lb_dg(s i32)` is exported and calls kdw(sl(s), dgs()). `lb_cp(s i32, d i32)` is exported and calls kcp(sl(d), sl(s)).

`lb_rnd(s i32)` is exported and fills slot s with the seeded start: for i from 0 below `N`: when `FX`[i] is not 0 or bad(`MV`[i]) is true, the entry becomes 0.0; otherwise p is (`SD` as real) times 1103515245.0 plus 12345.0, `SD` becomes ((p as i64) bitwise-and 2147483647) as int, and the entry becomes (`SD` as real) divided by 2147483647.0, minus 0.5.

`lb_orth(nv i32, ha i32, nb i32) i32` is exported. It orthogonalizes the slots `L1`[0] to `L1`[nv−1] (and, when ha is not 0, updates the matching slots `L2`[j] the same way) against the slots `L3`[0] to `L3`[nb−1] (whose matching slots are in `L4`) and against the ones it already kept, and returns how many it kept; their positions j go to `KO` in order. ko starts at 0. For j from 0 below nv: v is `L1`[j] as int and w is `L2`[j] as int; n0 is the square root of md(v, v); when n0 is above 0.0 is false (so also when n0 is NaN), j is skipped. Otherwise two passes run, and in each pass: for i from 0 below nb, with b equal to `L3`[i] as int, c is md(b, v), then ax(v, c, b), and when ha is not 0 also ax(w, c, `L4`[i] as int); then for i from 0 below ko, with o equal to `KO`[i] as int, c is md(`L1`[o] as int, v), then ax(v, c, `L1`[o] as int), and when ha is not 0 also ax(w, c, `L2`[o] as int). After the two passes nr is the square root of md(v, v); when nr is above 1e-9 times n0 is false, j is skipped. Otherwise it calls kdv(sl(v), nr), and when ha is not 0 also kdv(sl(w), nr); then `KO`[ko] becomes j and ko grows by 1. It returns ko.

`jac(m int)` is the cyclic Jacobi eigen solver on the m by m matrix in `GM` (entry row r, column c at `GM`[r·m + c]), accumulating rotations in `EV`. Every entry of `EV` from 0 below m·m becomes 0.0, then `EV`[i·m + i] becomes 1.0 for i from 0 below m. nf starts at 0.0 and for i from 0 below m·m becomes nf plus `GM`[i] times `GM`[i]. For sw from 0 below 80: off starts at 0.0 and, for p from 0 below m and q from p plus 1 below m, becomes off plus `GM`[p·m + q] times `GM`[p·m + q]; the sweeps stop when off is at most 1e-30 times nf. Then for p from 0 below m and q from p plus 1 below m: apq is `GM`[p·m + q]; when bad(apq) is true that pair is skipped; otherwise th is (`GM`[q·m + q] minus `GM`[p·m + p]) divided by (2.0 times apq), sg is 1.0 when th is at least 0.0 and −1.0 otherwise, t is sg divided by (the absolute value of th plus the square root of (th times th plus 1.0)), c is 1.0 divided by the square root of (t times t plus 1.0), and s is t times c. Then three loops over k from 0 below m, each finished before the next starts: first x is `GM`[k·m + p] and y is `GM`[k·m + q], `GM`[k·m + p] becomes c times x minus s times y and `GM`[k·m + q] becomes s times x plus c times y; second the same with x equal to `GM`[p·m + k] and y equal to `GM`[q·m + k], writing `GM`[p·m + k] and `GM`[q·m + k]; third the same with x equal to `EV`[k·m + p] and y equal to `EV`[k·m + q], writing `EV`[k·m + p] and `EV`[k·m + q].

`osort(m int)` sets `OD`[i] to i for i from 0 below m, then stably sorts those m entries ascending by the key `GM`[x·m + x] of each entry x, as a bottom-up merge sort that uses `SW` as scratch. w starts at 1; while w is below m: lo starts at 0; while lo is below m: mid is the smaller of lo plus w and m, hi is the smaller of lo plus 2·w and m; i is lo, j is mid and k is lo; while k is below hi: when j is below hi and (i equals mid or the key of `OD`[j] is below the key of `OD`[i]), `SW`[k] becomes `OD`[j] and j grows by 1; otherwise `SW`[k] becomes `OD`[i] and i grows by 1; k grows by 1. Then `OD`[x] becomes `SW`[x] for x from lo below hi, and lo becomes hi. Then w doubles.

`comb(y int, ls, j int, f0 int, m int)` takes an i32 growable array ls by reference and writes into slot y the combination of the slots ls[f0] to ls[m−1] with the coefficients in column j of `CC`: it calls kz(sl(y)); then for i from f0 below m: c is `CC`[i·m + j]; when bad(c) is true, i is skipped; otherwise it calls kad(sl(y), c, sl(ls[i] as int)).

`lb_rr(m0 i32, kp0 i32, k0 i32)` is exported and is the Rayleigh-Ritz step on the m0 slots in `L1` (the basis) and the m0 slots in `L2` (the operator applied to it). With m, kp and k equal to m0, kp0 and k0 as int: for i from 0 below m and j from i below m, g is (dt(`L1`[i] as int, `L2`[j] as int) plus dt(`L1`[j] as int, `L2`[i] as int)) divided by 2.0, and both `GM`[i·m + j] and `GM`[j·m + i] become g. Then jac(m) and osort(m). Then `CC`[i·m + j] becomes `EV`[i·m + (`OD`[j] as int)] for i and j from 0 below m. Then for j from 0 below k, with o equal to `OD`[j] as int, `LM`[j] becomes `GM`[o·m + o]. Then for j from 0 below k: comb(`L3`[j] as int, `L1`, j, 0, m) and comb(`L3`[k + j] as int, `L2`, j, 0, m), and when kp is above 0 also comb(`L4`[j] as int, `L1`, j, kp, m) and comb(`L4`[k + j] as int, `L2`, j, kp, m).

`lb_res(nx i32)` is exported and writes residuals: for j from 0 below nx as int, with x equal to `L1`[j] as int, a equal to `L2`[j] as int and r equal to `L3`[j] as int: it calls krs(sl(r), sl(a), sl(x), mvs(), `LM`[j]); then s is knr(sl(r), mvs()); then `RS`[j] becomes (the square root of s) divided by jmax(the absolute value of `LM`[j], 1e-30).

`main()` does nothing.
